-The mechanics of that master's study are worth recording because the dissertation's torque work reuses them. Moment arms were computed geometrically from attachment points in the body frame, as the cross product of a joint-axis unit vector with the vector from the joint center to the muscle's line of action, the same construction described in Section~\ref{sec:bg_biomechanics}. PAM routings were then placed by hand in OpenSim beside the Gait2392 paths they imitate, moved to respect Festo BPA contraction limits and to keep actuators from interfering with one another, and the result was saved as the gait2392\_robotbody model that the later chapters of this dissertation continue to refine \citep{bolen_bipedal_2019}. Isometric torque followed as muscle force multiplied by moment arm, evaluated across each joint's range of motion. The biceps femoris long head shows what the comparison exposed: its path differs visibly between the two models as the knee flexes, and the robot model's knee- and hip-axis torque surfaces reach only a fraction of the human model's peaks over the hip--knee range. Profiles of this kind, read across the full muscle set, defined the matching problem that the placement optimization later formalized \citep{morrow_optimization_2020}. Figure~\ref{fig:masters_routing_and_arms} shows the routing models and the moment-arm construction, and Figure~\ref{fig:masters_torque_comparison} shows the biceps femoris comparison.
+The mechanics of that master's study are worth recording because the dissertation's torque work reuses them. Moment arms were computed geometrically from attachment points in the body frame, as the cross product of a joint-axis unit vector with the vector from the joint center to the muscle's line of action, the same construction described in Section~\ref{sec:bg_biomechanics}. PAM routings were then placed by hand in OpenSim beside the Gait2392 paths they imitate, moved to respect Festo BPA contraction limits and to keep actuators from interfering with one another, and the result was saved as the gait2392\_robotbody model that the later chapters of this dissertation continue to refine \citep{bolen_bipedal_2019}. Isometric torque followed as muscle force multiplied by moment arm, evaluated across each joint's range of motion. The biceps femoris long head shows what the comparison exposed: its path differs visibly between the two models as the knee flexes, and the robot model's knee- and hip-axis torque surfaces reach only a fraction of the human model's peaks over the hip--knee range. Profiles of this kind, read across the full muscle set, defined the matching problem that the placement optimization later formalized. Figure~\ref{fig:masters_routing_and_arms} shows the routing models and the moment-arm construction, and Figure~\ref{fig:masters_torque_comparison} shows the biceps femoris comparison.
 
